<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Include xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">

<!-- The three values that have to move together on every release live here and nowhere else: the
     product version, the product code and the upgrade code. ObjectARXWizards.wxs (the MSI) and
     Bundle.wxs (the Burn bundle) both <?include?> this file, so the bundle cannot carry a version of
     its own that drifts from the Identity it chains (MsiSetup.cs warns about exactly that mistake for
     the ProductCode).
     The version and the code move together, as always: reusing a code with a different version is
     rejected by msiexec with error 1638 ("another version of this product is already installed"), and
     reusing it with the same version can only ever be a repair of the package that is already there -
     a repeat run of the same build shows the maintenance page instead of upgrading. -->
<?define RTMProductVersion = "18.0.0"?>
<!-- The first one - do not edit. -->
<?define ProductVersion = "0.2.15"?>
<?define UpgradeCode = "{A5569809-8D74-410A-83C3-F3AB0C4E2F80}"?>

<!-- Historic product codes, kept for reference; the live one is the last define in this file. -->
<!--UpgradeCode GUID "{7092FED1-4FCC-4565-A8B6-44DCD4583F92}"?> from a retired per-year release-->
<!--Product GUID "{8046A89B-41DD-4779-A741-64A601603076}"?> from a retired per-year release-->
<!--ProductCode from a retired per-year release-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{AB65E222-EAB0-494E-AC65-07BF30A915F3}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode from a retired per-year release-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{2F8A7D4E-B3C6-4E91-A8F2-7D5E9C1A6B48}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.1, the first build of the restarted numbering.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{922142B3-22ED-4543-A8AB-E2AF5820105F}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.2, which brought the MSI in line with the Inno line: one Autodesk root
    instead of separate SDK/AutoCAD roots and no separate AutoCAD location, a selectable 64-bit
    install folder, the property sheet folder following it, an empty-by-default RDS, and a Chinese
    wizard.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{D8C4DE84-665B-4442-85C0-2DE1C293EBE0}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.3, which carries the VSIX fixes (the wizard templates named a 26.x assembly,
    and new resource ids were defined below the resource editor's bookkeeping block).-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{2D2AA7F2-1804-4816-ADEF-1932D00CB77C}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.4, which renames the product to Multi-Version, stops shipping the
    authoring machine's experimental property sheets, and remembers the previous install folder.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{4E9C1F38-6A2D-4B07-9C51-83D7E0A4B6F2}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.5, which carries the real wizard icons (the VSIX shipped a 60-byte stub
    next to every template, so the Add Class and New Project lists had no visual distinction).-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{A7F2C4E8-5D31-49B6-8E07-C62B1F4A9D53}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.6, which drops the retired "Trial" configuration from the property sheets
    the installer generates.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{C31D7B05-9E64-4A2F-8B7D-5F0A6E2C9D14}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.1.7, whose project templates set ProvideDefaultName=false so VS does not run
    the NewProjectDialog.UpdateNameField -> GenerateItemName call that crashes the IDE (see docs 18).
    ProductVersion and ProductCode have to move together, and both have to move for every build that
    is meant to go on top of an existing install: reusing this code with a different version is
    rejected by msiexec with error 1638 ("another version of this product is already installed"), and
    reusing it with the same version can only ever be a repair of the package that is already there -
    it would show the maintenance page instead of upgrading.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{E8B46D29-7C15-4F83-A6E2-9D5B3C71F0A8}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.6. It exists because the bundle got a fixed Id (see Bundle.wxs): rebuilding the
    bundle no longer produces a new identity, so this is the release that replaces the 0.2.4/0.2.5 rows
    instead of adding another one. The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{8D2A5F47-6C1E-4B93-A072-5F3E9D81C4B6}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.9, which drops the ObjectARX SDK location: the installer no longer
    writes into the SDK's inc folder (the year sheets import the SDK's own inc\dbx.props and
    carry the two CRX pieces crx.props used to add). The ARXPATH field, its property, the
    permanent inc component and the two dead payload copies in the install folder go with it.
    The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{4F2ADFF1-6B7E-4E10-BC73-E65E2B4F3B54}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.10, which records the RDS the setup was given so the VSIX project
    wizard can start from it (it reads HKLM\SOFTWARE\Autodesk\ObjectARX Wizards\RDS). The
    version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{C555DCC8-44C8-490D-9A8A-3CF8EEBC5B93}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.11, which drops the classic wizard sources and _Installs payload from
    the branch and the CA helpers that only patched them; the MSI still ships only the generated
    props and the VSIX is still the whole Visual Studio integration. The version and the code
    move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{17F6C7D0-89F3-43B8-8640-E2B386775EA1}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.12, which splits the one Autodesk root into two: the paths page now asks for the Autodesk CAD root and the ObjectARX SDK root separately and drops the property-sheet field (it always followed the install folder). The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{C3306051-5B8F-47FD-A8F7-D6E0B7D7E641}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.13, which fixes the VSIX project wizard Target Versions presets
    (All / None / Invert / Compatible had shrunk to two buttons) and defaults the MFC
    dialog-resource option to on. The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{8937E58A-F779-418D-B4A8-E47C66B56268}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.14. The bundle no longer asks a registry key whether the MSI is present: both
    packages now detect this exact product code (util:ProductSearch in Bundle.wxs). The old test read
    "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Autodesk\ObjectARX Wizards", a key with no version in it, so on an upgrade the
    previous build's key was still there during planning: Burn judged both packages already installed,
    skipped them, and the previous bundle's uninstall took the rest away - the generated property
    sheets and the year selection disappeared and the VSIX stayed at the old build (2026-10-20). A
    product code that is unique per build makes the state unambiguous: absent until it really lands.
    The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<!--<?define ProductCode= "{0D09B7EA-A6E6-4BEE-95E5-763A2F39DDA8}"?>-->
<!--ProductCode for 0.2.15, which drops the MFC class wizard's "Create dialog resource in the
    project" checkbox. The flag never changed what the wizard did - the .rc / Resource.h step keys
    off "the project does not define this id yet", not off the flag - so the generated class was
    identical either way and the switch only made the page ask a question that had no answer.
    The version and the code move together, as always.-->
<?define ProductCode= "{B74BCA5E-8476-45D5-8431-A272717A33F9}"?>

</Include>
